2026-06-30 15:40:36,856 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-3939a247-1fcc-453c-ba36-d371e5dbcf0d', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n210430_HSPB5_B9_noUV_01.raw\n210430_HSPB5_B9_yesUV_01.raw\n210727_HSPB5_B9_noUV_T_mon_01.raw\n210727_HSPB5_B9_yesUV_T_dim_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B17_noUV_mon_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B17_yesUV_dim_65_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B17_yesUV_dim_75_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B24_noUV_mon_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B24_yesUV_dim_65_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B24_yesUV_dim_75_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B33_noUV_mon_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B33_yesUV_dim_75_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B47_noUV_mon_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B47_yesUV_dim_65_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B47_yesUV_dim_75_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B61_noUV_mon_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B61_yesUV_dim_65_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B61_yesUV_dim_75_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B9_noUV_mon_TG_02.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B9_yesUV_dim_65_TG_02.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B9_yesUV_dim_75_TG_01.raw\n211014_HSPB5_B9_yesUV_dim_T_std_APD_02.raw\n230512_HSPB5_B9_pH65_dim10_01_inj7.raw\n230512_HSPB5_B9_pH65_dim10_02_inj8.raw\n230512_HSPB5_B9_pH65_dim10_03_inj9.raw\n230512_HSPB5_B9_pH65_dim2_01_inj1.raw\n230512_HSPB5_B9_pH65_dim2_02_inj2.raw\n230512_HSPB5_B9_pH65_dim2_03_inj3.raw\n230512_HSPB5_B9_pH65_dim8_01_inj4.raw\n230512_HSPB5_B9_pH65_dim8_02_inj5.raw\n230512_HSPB5_B9_pH65_dim8_03_inj6.raw\n230512_HSPB5_B9_yesUV_dim_T_olderstd_30min_01.raw\n230512_HSPB5_B9_yesUV_dim_T_olderstd_90min_01.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nA High-Performance Workflow for Identifying Site-Specific Crosslinks Originating from a Genetically Incorporated, Photoreactive Amino Acid\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nIn conventional crosslinking mass spectrometry, proteins are crosslinked using a highly selective, bifunctional chemical reagent, which limits crosslinks to residues that are accessible and reactive to the reagent. Genetically incorporating a photoreactive amino acid offers two key advantages: any site can be targeted, including those that are inaccessible to conventional crosslinking reagents, and photoreactive amino acids can potentially react with a broad range of interaction partners. However, broad reactivity imposes additional challenges for crosslink identification. In this study, we incorporate benzoylphenylalanine (BPA), a photoreactive amino acid, at selected sites in an intrinsically disordered region of the human protein HSPB5. We report and characterize a workflow for identifying and visualizing residue-level interactions originating from BPA. We routinely identify 30 to 300 crosslinked peptide spectral matches with this workflow, which is up to ten times more than existing tools for residue-level BPA crosslink identification. Most identified crosslinks are assigned to a precision of one or two residues, which is supported by a high degree of overlap between replicate analyses. Based on these results, we anticipate that this workflow will support the more general use of genetically incorporated, photoreactive amino acids for characterizing the structures of proteins that have resisted high-resolution characterization.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe HSPB5-BPA variants W9B, F17B, F24B, L33B, F47B, and F61B were prepared in BL21 E. coli cells using an amber codon suppression. Purified BPA-containing variants were UV-treated to form crosslinks, and the product mixtures were subjected to SDS-PAGE on a precast 4–20% acrylamide gradient gel (Bio-Rad, 4561096). The monomeric reactants (proteins in the monomer band from the non-UV treated sample) and dimeric products (proteins in the dimer band from UV-treated sample) were excised and were each digested in-gel following the procedure for the Thermo In-Gel Digestion Kit. The weight (μg) of protein loaded onto the gel and the relative color intensity of the band excised from the gel prior to digestion was used to estimate the weight of the digested peptides loaded on the column. For example, when 7.6 μg of protein was loaded onto a gel lane and the dimeric product band was roughly 30% of the total color intensity, we estimated that the excised dimeric products contained about 2.3 μg of protein. In that case, about 20% (500 ug) of the digested peptides were loaded on the column. For trypsin-GluC-digested samples, GluC and trypsin were each added to the digestion buffer at 0.004 mg∙mL–1. Samples were digested overnight at 30 ºC, and then prepared for LC-MS using C18 spin columns (Thermo Scientific Pierce, 89870). \nSamples were analyzed using an Easy Nano LC coupled to a Thermo Orbitrap Fusion Lumos Tribrid and data-dependent acquisition. Roughly 500 ng of peptide was loaded onto an 8-cm trap column. The sample was then then separated on a 25-cm analytical column with a 75 µm inner diameter using an 85-minute or 30-minute gradient from 6% B to 45% B, where A was water and B was 80% acetonitrile, at 300 nL·min–1. The column was then flushed and regenerated for 35 minutes. Spectra were acquired across the entire LC method using data-dependent acquisition with dynamic exclusion after one time for a duration of 30 seconds and an intensity threshold of 2.0x104. Orbitrap detection and Higher-energy collisional dissociation (HCD) fragmentation (30% normalized collision energy) were used with a target value of 1.00x105, maximum injection time of 22 ms, top N of 20, and isolation width of 1.6. MS1 were acquired at a resolving power of 120,000 over the range of 400-2000 m/z, and MS2 were acquired with a resolution of 15,000.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nEscherichia coli; Homo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
