2026-06-30 15:40:54,569 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-92da4cfe-07fc-4b38-8c07-865c876e1582', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the mass spectrometry acquisition.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: instrument\nsdrf_column: comment[instrument]\nontology: MS:1000031 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Mass spectrometer instrument make and model.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology. If multiple instruments, return a list.\n  Common instruments (name → accession):\n    Orbitrap Astral → MS:1003356\n    Orbitrap Exploris 480 → MS:1003028\n    Orbitrap Eclipse → MS:1003349\n    Orbitrap Fusion Lumos → MS:1002732\n    Orbitrap Fusion → MS:1002416\n    Q Exactive HF-X → MS:1002877\n    Q Exactive HF → MS:1002523\n    Q Exactive Plus → MS:1002634\n    Q Exactive → MS:1001911\n    LTQ Orbitrap Velos → MS:1001742\n    LTQ Orbitrap XL → MS:1000556\n    timsTOF → MS:1003005\n    timsTOF Pro → MS:1003230\n    TripleTOF 6600 → MS:1002583\n  Format: NT=<instrument name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: proteomics data acquisition method\nsdrf_column: comment[proteomics data acquisition method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000659\ndescription: Mass spectrometry data acquisition method.\ninstructions: For XL-MS this is nearly always DDA. Use:\n  NT=data-dependent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000041 — for DDA/shotgun\n  NT=data-independent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000449 — for DIA (rare in XL-MS)\n  NT=targeted acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000450 — for SRM/PRM\n  Default to DDA if not stated.\n\nterm: dissociation method\nsdrf_column: comment[dissociation method]\nontology: MS:1000044 (PSI-MS children)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nA High-Performance Workflow for Identifying Site-Specific Crosslinks Originating from a Genetically Incorporated, Photoreactive Amino Acid\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nIn conventional crosslinking mass spectrometry, proteins are crosslinked using a highly selective, bifunctional chemical reagent, which limits crosslinks to residues that are accessible and reactive to the reagent. Genetically incorporating a photoreactive amino acid offers two key advantages: any site can be targeted, including those that are inaccessible to conventional crosslinking reagents, and photoreactive amino acids can potentially react with a broad range of interaction partners. However, broad reactivity imposes additional challenges for crosslink identification. In this study, we incorporate benzoylphenylalanine (BPA), a photoreactive amino acid, at selected sites in an intrinsically disordered region of the human protein HSPB5. We report and characterize a workflow for identifying and visualizing residue-level interactions originating from BPA. We routinely identify 30 to 300 crosslinked peptide spectral matches with this workflow, which is up to ten times more than existing tools for residue-level BPA crosslink identification. Most identified crosslinks are assigned to a precision of one or two residues, which is supported by a high degree of overlap between replicate analyses. Based on these results, we anticipate that this workflow will support the more general use of genetically incorporated, photoreactive amino acids for characterizing the structures of proteins that have resisted high-resolution characterization.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe HSPB5-BPA variants W9B, F17B, F24B, L33B, F47B, and F61B were prepared in BL21 E. coli cells using an amber codon suppression. Purified BPA-containing variants were UV-treated to form crosslinks, and the product mixtures were subjected to SDS-PAGE on a precast 4–20% acrylamide gradient gel (Bio-Rad, 4561096). The monomeric reactants (proteins in the monomer band from the non-UV treated sample) and dimeric products (proteins in the dimer band from UV-treated sample) were excised and were each digested in-gel following the procedure for the Thermo In-Gel Digestion Kit. The weight (μg) of protein loaded onto the gel and the relative color intensity of the band excised from the gel prior to digestion was used to estimate the weight of the digested peptides loaded on the column. For example, when 7.6 μg of protein was loaded onto a gel lane and the dimeric product band was roughly 30% of the total color intensity, we estimated that the excised dimeric products contained about 2.3 μg of protein. In that case, about 20% (500 ug) of the digested peptides were loaded on the column. For trypsin-GluC-digested samples, GluC and trypsin were each added to the digestion buffer at 0.004 mg∙mL–1. Samples were digested overnight at 30 ºC, and then prepared for LC-MS using C18 spin columns (Thermo Scientific Pierce, 89870). \nSamples were analyzed using an Easy Nano LC coupled to a Thermo Orbitrap Fusion Lumos Tribrid and data-dependent acquisition. Roughly 500 ng of peptide was loaded onto an 8-cm trap column. The sample was then then separated on a 25-cm analytical column with a 75 µm inner diameter using an 85-minute or 30-minute gradient from 6% B to 45% B, where A was water and B was 80% acetonitrile, at 300 nL·min–1. The column was then flushed and regenerated for 35 minutes. Spectra were acquired across the entire LC method using data-dependent acquisition with dynamic exclusion after one time for a duration of 30 seconds and an intensity threshold of 2.0x104. Orbitrap detection and Higher-energy collisional dissociation (HCD) fragmentation (30% normalized collision energy) were used with a target value of 1.00x105, maximum injection time of 22 ms, top N of 20, and isolation width of 1.6. MS1 were acquired at a resolving power of 120,000 over the range of 400-2000 m/z, and MS2 were acquired with a resolution of 15,000.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
