2026-06-30 15:40:58,767 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-b2390975-70f7-4816-82fe-e6c2e8bcaeb9', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\nFor each term, return the best available ontology-resolved value with its accession where indicated.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: organism\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism]\nontology: NCBITaxon (e.g. NCBITaxon:9606)\ndescription: Source species of the proteome of interest in the biological samples analyzed by mass spectrometry. Return the scientific name. \nSource species maybe different than host species when proteins from one organism are expressed in another. \ninstructions: Use NCBITaxon accession. Common examples: Homo sapiens (NCBITaxon:9606),\n  Mus musculus (NCBITaxon:10090), Saccharomyces cerevisiae (NCBITaxon:4932),\n  Escherichia coli (NCBITaxon:562). If multiple species, return a list.\n\nterm: organism part\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism part]\nontology: UBERON or BTO\ndescription: Main normalized anatomical term for the sample analyzed by mass spectrometry. \ninstructions: Use the clearest ontology-backed anatomy term. Examples: whole-cell lysate, mitochondria,\n  nucleus, cytoplasm, liver, heart, brain, cell, ect. Return null if not applicable\n  (e.g. purified recombinant proteins with no tissue origin).\n\nterm: cell line\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell line]\nontology: CLO, BTO, or EFO (e.g. CLO:0001230 for HEK293)\ndescription: Name of the cell line if a cell line was used.\ninstructions: Return null if no cell line was used (primary cells or tissues).\n\nterm: cell type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell type]\nontology: CL, BTO, or CLO\ndescription: Cell type of the sample.\ninstructions: Return null if not applicable or not stated.\n\nterm: material type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[material type]\nontology: PRIDE:0000837\ndescription: Type of biological material analyzed. Differentiates whether the whole cell lysate was analyzed, just a specific anatomical structure or tissue, or if the samples were purified for specific proteins before mass spectrometry analysis. \nallowed_values: tissue, whole cell lysate, organell, membrane, synthetic, purified proteins, affinity captured\ninstructions: Choose the best matching fixed value. Return null if unclear.\n\nterm: strain or breed\nsdrf_column: characteristics[strain or breed]\nontology: free text\ndescription: Strain, breed, cultivar, or ecotype of the organism.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: developmental stage\nsdrf_column: characteristics[developmental stage]\nontology: EFO\ndescription: Developmental stage of the organism or donor.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: technology type\nsdrf_column: technology type\nontology: PRIDE:0000663\ndescription: Type of technology used. For XL-MS this is always fixed.\nallowed_values: proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry" for XL-MS datasets.\n\nterm: source name\nsdrf_column: source name\nontology: PRIDE:0000623\ndescription: Unique identifier for the biological sample. Typically derived from\n  the dataset file names or sample descriptions in the paper.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention used (e.g. "PXD_stem" or "condition_replicate").\n  This is auto-generated from file names; return a description of the sample naming pattern only.\n\nterm: assay name\nsdrf_column: assay name\nontology: NCIT:C178857\ndescription: Unique identifier for each MS run/assay. Typically the raw file stem.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention. This is auto-generated; return the pattern only.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable (e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"), else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text supporting the extraction, or null\n  "evidence_location": where in the paper (e.g. "Methods", "Abstract"), or null\n\nExample output structure:\n{\n  "characteristics[organism]": {"value": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae", "accession": "NCBITaxon:4932", "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  "characteristics[organism part]": {"value": "mitochondria", "accession": null, "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  ...\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nA High-Performance Workflow for Identifying Site-Specific Crosslinks Originating from a Genetically Incorporated, Photoreactive Amino Acid\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nIn conventional crosslinking mass spectrometry, proteins are crosslinked using a highly selective, bifunctional chemical reagent, which limits crosslinks to residues that are accessible and reactive to the reagent. Genetically incorporating a photoreactive amino acid offers two key advantages: any site can be targeted, including those that are inaccessible to conventional crosslinking reagents, and photoreactive amino acids can potentially react with a broad range of interaction partners. However, broad reactivity imposes additional challenges for crosslink identification. In this study, we incorporate benzoylphenylalanine (BPA), a photoreactive amino acid, at selected sites in an intrinsically disordered region of the human protein HSPB5. We report and characterize a workflow for identifying and visualizing residue-level interactions originating from BPA. We routinely identify 30 to 300 crosslinked peptide spectral matches with this workflow, which is up to ten times more than existing tools for residue-level BPA crosslink identification. Most identified crosslinks are assigned to a precision of one or two residues, which is supported by a high degree of overlap between replicate analyses. Based on these results, we anticipate that this workflow will support the more general use of genetically incorporated, photoreactive amino acids for characterizing the structures of proteins that have resisted high-resolution characterization.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe HSPB5-BPA variants W9B, F17B, F24B, L33B, F47B, and F61B were prepared in BL21 E. coli cells using an amber codon suppression. Purified BPA-containing variants were UV-treated to form crosslinks, and the product mixtures were subjected to SDS-PAGE on a precast 4–20% acrylamide gradient gel (Bio-Rad, 4561096). The monomeric reactants (proteins in the monomer band from the non-UV treated sample) and dimeric products (proteins in the dimer band from UV-treated sample) were excised and were each digested in-gel following the procedure for the Thermo In-Gel Digestion Kit. The weight (μg) of protein loaded onto the gel and the relative color intensity of the band excised from the gel prior to digestion was used to estimate the weight of the digested peptides loaded on the column. For example, when 7.6 μg of protein was loaded onto a gel lane and the dimeric product band was roughly 30% of the total color intensity, we estimated that the excised dimeric products contained about 2.3 μg of protein. In that case, about 20% (500 ug) of the digested peptides were loaded on the column. For trypsin-GluC-digested samples, GluC and trypsin were each added to the digestion buffer at 0.004 mg∙mL–1. Samples were digested overnight at 30 ºC, and then prepared for LC-MS using C18 spin columns (Thermo Scientific Pierce, 89870). \nSamples were analyzed using an Easy Nano LC coupled to a Thermo Orbitrap Fusion Lumos Tribrid and data-dependent acquisition. Roughly 500 ng of peptide was loaded onto an 8-cm trap column. The sample was then then separated on a 25-cm analytical column with a 75 µm inner diameter using an 85-minute or 30-minute gradient from 6% B to 45% B, where A was water and B was 80% acetonitrile, at 300 nL·min–1. The column was then flushed and regenerated for 35 minutes. Spectra were acquired across the entire LC method using data-dependent acquisition with dynamic exclusion after one time for a duration of 30 seconds and an intensity threshold of 2.0x104. Orbitrap detection and Higher-energy collisional dissociation (HCD) fragmentation (30% normalized collision energy) were used with a target value of 1.00x105, maximum injection time of 22 ms, top N of 20, and isolation width of 1.6. MS1 were acquired at a resolving power of 120,000 over the range of 400-2000 m/z, and MS2 were acquired with a resolution of 15,000.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
